78em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14.0</span></span></span></span>","key":"dCBIwb9Mrw"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"seJPMdHaIe"}],"key":"wbzmeYefQB"}],"key":"G7c8zrIODs"}],"key":"IvVCkzpXV1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Below we present two possible scenarios for our example. In both cases, we construct side-by-side boxplots for four groups of frustration levels that have the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"AZw0ZqDFvI"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"same","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"QPnkfQVVyY"}],"key":"Xb1SlL7ppT"},{"type":"text","value":" variation among their means. Thus, scenario 1 and scenario 2 both show data for four groups with the sample means 7.3, 11.8, 13.2, and 14.0 (indicated with red marks).","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"lhukJPVt3r"}],"key":"zdavXk5AbG"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m16-anova-scenario1-becd95099c8c9061f0d8bf5c0becb68f.svg","alt":"Side-by-side boxplots for the four majors in which each box is very tall—the interquartile ranges span 10 or more frustration points, and the boxes overlap heavily. Every major's mean lies within the box of every other major.","key":"PtGiL572Vc","urlSource":"images/gen/m16-anova-scenario1.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"DWFaCWLbvd"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m16-anova-scenario2-dea2e40b83f8276c1221bfcf4f0e6103.svg","alt":"Side-by-side boxplots for the four majors with the same means, but each box is much shorter. The Business boxplot barely overlaps the other three, whose means lie well outside its box.","key":"c7fi4GKIsa","urlSource":"images/gen/m16-anova-scenario2.svg"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"cmexdDhRB0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The important difference between the two scenarios is that the first represents data with a ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"QaCH3NKx6g"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"large","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"u9YKtzT1GR"}],"key":"kG4mkJqxa2"},{"type":"text","value":" amount of variation within each of the four groups, while the second represents data with a ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"Hdv7lGBUdJ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"small","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"gPlhESmn83"}],"key":"KS6x6APejW"},{"type":"text","value":" amount of variation within each of the four groups.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"EYpzIM1il7"}],"key":"NwpWwkATkC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scenario 1, because of the large amount of spread within the groups, shows boxplots with plenty of overlap. One could imagine the data arising from 4 random samples taken from 4 populations all having the same mean of about 11 or 12. The first group of values may have been a bit on the low side, and the other three a bit on the high side, but such differences could conceivably have come about by chance. This would be the case if the null hypothesis, claiming equal population means, were true.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"lZkyxMW57Y"}],"key":"PdoxTpFvbl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scenario 2, because of the small amount of spread within the groups, shows boxplots with very little overlap. It would be very hard to believe that we are sampling from four groups that have equal population means. This would be the case if the null hypothesis, claiming equal population means, were false.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"C3OPR37VyO"}],"key":"wrRWcwm6p7"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Thus, in the language of hypothesis tests, we would say that if the data were configured as they are in scenario 1, we would not reject the null hypothesis that population mean frustration levels are equal for the four majors. If the data were configured as they are in scenario 2, we would reject the null hypothesis, and we would conclude that mean frustration levels differ depending on major.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"HnB4YaOCC3"}],"key":"SF2OSwVJBI"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: The Idea Behind the F-Test","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"HGPsgIUJZB"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-the-idea-behind-the-f-test","label":"Check Your Understanding: The Idea Behind the F-Test","html_id":"check-your-understanding-the-idea-behind-the-f-test","implicit":true,"key":"yeKgdt6SRt"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-68378715a95f021281d81f443f976cac.mjs","model":{"question":"The sample means are identical in both scenarios, yet they lead to opposite conclusions. What explains this?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The within-group variation differs—the same differences among means are convincing only when within-group spread is small","feedback":"Correct! The strength of the evidence depends on how large the between-group differences are RELATIVE to the within-group variation—large within-group spread (scenario 1) makes the same mean differences unconvincing."},{"correct":false,"text":"The between-group variation differs between the scenarios","feedback":"The sample means (and hence the between-group variation) are the same in both scenarios."},{"correct":false,"text":"The sample sizes differ between the scenarios","feedback":"The sample sizes are 35 per group in both scenarios."}],"multi":false,"hint":"What differs between the two scenarios?","explanation":""},"key":"zgGJsY72dX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s summarize what we learned from this. The question we need to answer is: ","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"ji9aijTqPv"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"are the differences among the sample means due to true differences among the μ’s (alternative hypothesis), or merely due to sampling variability (null hypothesis)?","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"nZWDPGRHZu"}],"key":"TiTIcyAUPE"}],"key":"k3lHvlTSkk"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In order to answer this question using our data, we obviously need to look at the variation among the sample means, but this alone is not enough. We need to look at the variation among the sample means ","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"aTVVoIje3J"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"relative to","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"MUoVVWT1R7"}],"key":"rgbjvlwFki"},{"type":"text","value":" the variation within the groups. In other words, we need to look at the quantity:","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"eVHQuq6m6t"}],"key":"UjE01kPrFX"},{"type":"math","value":"\\frac{\\text{variation among sample means}}{\\text{variation within groups}}","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mtext>variation among sample means</mtext><mtext>variation within groups</mtext></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\text{variation among sample means}}{\\text{variation within groups}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">variation within groups</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">variation among sample means</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span>","enumerator":"1","key":"KG9iRgyXoh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"which measures to what extent the difference among the sampled groups’ means dominates over the usual variation within sampled groups (which reflects differences in individuals that are typical in random samples).","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"pTPDdnH0GU"}],"key":"pxArxGN85m"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the variation within groups is large (like in scenario 1), the variation (differences) among the sample means could become negligible, and the data provide very little evidence against ","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"DrmapsHCuT"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"b0B80yele7"},{"type":"text","value":". When the variation within groups is small (like in scenario 2), the variation among the sample means dominates over it, and the data have stronger evidence against ","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"by8ghFlMw5"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"YYfO9J5nYQ"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"NxfFHW7w4A"}],"key":"WAZOrc44hm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Looking at this ratio of variations is the idea behind comparing more than two means; hence the name ","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"WcM8YtuvR8"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"analysis of variance","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"TSqMh8YFgj"}],"key":"Or9Y5wToXh"},{"type":"text","value":" (ANOVA).","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"E5DYlS0X2A"}],"key":"FmcH73fgDT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we understand the idea behind the ANOVA F-test, let’s move on to step 2. We’ll start by talking about the test statistic, since it will be a natural continuation of what we’ve just discussed, and then move on to talk about the conditions under which the ANOVA F-test can be used. In practice, however, the conditions need to be checked first, as we did before.","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"rrngfmfodw"}],"key":"VcfqgjtbOe"}],"key":"H0ZI4bgGDG"}],"key":"nBSzc7H8Yl"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The ANOVA F-Test: Stating the Hypotheses","url":"/pages/m16-anova-2-of-7","group":"Section 13: Inference for Relationships"},"next":{"title":"The F-Statistic: Conditions and Computation","url":"/pages/m16-anova-4-of-7","group":"Section 13: Inference for Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}